WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:05.680
A few weeks ago I released
videos on DEET vs. natural repellant

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:07.760
that we can apply
to our clothes or skin,

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:13.200
but can things we eat
also have a repellant quality?

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:17.040
No word yet on vampires,
but... we do have evidence

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:21.020
on garlic and other blood suckers.
Check it out...

00:00:21.325 --> 00:00:31.006
"Friday Favorites: Do Garlic, Beer, Bananas,
and B Vitamins Really Repel Mosquitoes?"

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:34.800
Are some people just more
attractive to mosquitos than others?

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:36.720
Apparently so,
as identical twins

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:39.600
are most likely
to be similarly tasty

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:41.920
compared to fraternal
twins who only share

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:43.600
50 percent of their DNA,

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:48.080
demonstrating an underlying genetic
component that can be sniffed out

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:50.560
by mosquitoes, although it’s
not clear if it’s because

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:53.840
some people smell better
or other people just smell worse.

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:57.680
We know pregnant women are twice
as attractive to malaria mosquitoes

00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:00.880
and also that mosquitoes
are attracted to sweat.

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:04.800
Human sweat contains components
that are attractive to anthropophilic—

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:07.760
meaning human-loving—
mosquitoes.

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:10.960
The unique composition of human
sweat appears to explain

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:14.320
its tantalizing effect, though sweat
from some body parts

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:16.960
are evidently more
tantalizing than others.

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:19.360
Skin emanations
collected from armpits

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:21.920
were less attractive
compared to hands or feet.

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:25.200
Here’s the graph. They think
the difference may be caused

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:29.040
by deodorant residues,
since in a subsequent experiment,

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:32.480
volunteers were asked to avoid using
skincare products for five days,

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:36.080
and after that no
differences were detected.

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:39.840
The creepiest bit
of research I found was this.

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:44.000
The parasites that caused
the mosquito-borne disease malaria,

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:46.560
which kills hundreds
of thousands of people a year,

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:50.160
makes you more
attractive to, you guessed it,

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:53.600
the mosquitoes
that transmit malaria.

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:57.200
What better way for the parasites
to hitch from person-to-person.

00:01:57.200 --> 00:01:58.880
Instead of leaving
it up to chance,

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:02.720
some parasites manipulate
their hosts in elaborate ways.

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:06.720
I’ve talked about how the toxoplasma
brain parasite draws mice

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:10.720
to the smell of cat urine
to get into cats’ brains,

00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:14.160
or how about the rabies virus
concentrating in the saliva

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:18.400
while tapping into the Cujo
rage circuit in the brain.

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:22.480
There’s even a parasite that needs
to get from an ant to a bird;

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:27.360
so, it turns the ant’s belly bright
red to look like a ripe berry

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:29.200
and makes the ant
stick it up into

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:32.400
the air to confuse
fruit-eating bird.

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:35.200
Here’s the ants before
and after infection.

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:37.760
Just like malaria
parasites making us

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:41.040
particularly tasty
to mosquitos.

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:44.000
Anything we can do to make
ourselves less tasty?

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:46.320
I’ve talked about the various
mosquito repellents

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:47.840
you could spray on your skin,

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:51.360
both synthetic and natural
mosquito repellents,

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:52.560
but is there
anything you can eat

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:56.480
or drink to make you
less of a target?

00:02:56.480 --> 00:03:00.000
When you search the scientific
literature for diet and mosquitoes,

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:04.560
a lot of articles like this
pop up, on diets for mosquitoes,

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:10.080
like SkitoSnack for that artificial
blood meal replacement.

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:14.000
Feeding mice different
diets makes a difference,

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:16.240
but what about people?

00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:17.600
One of the most
common anecdotes

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:20.000
is that vitamin B
complex supplements

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:21.920
protect against biting insects.

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:26.080
Other anecdotal remedies include
the taking of garlic, marmite,

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:29.440
vegemite, brewer’s yeast
tablets, and alcohol,

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:34.640
but you don’t know...
until you put it to the test.

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:37.200
How about a randomized, double-blind,
placebo-controlled trial

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:39.360
of garlic as a mosquito repellant?

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:40.960
A belief in the ability of garlic

00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:43.280
to repel insects seems
to be widespread;

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:47.520
so, researchers in Connecticut
decided to see if it were true.

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:49.120
They looked at the numbers
of mosquitoes

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:50.880
that did not feed
on the subjects,

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:52.240
the number of mosquito bites,

00:03:52.240 --> 00:03:54.160
the weights of the mosquitoes
after feeding,

00:03:54.160 --> 00:03:56.720
and the amount of blood
ingested were all determined

00:03:56.720 --> 00:03:59.040
on people on and off garlic,

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:02.400
and the data did not
provide evidence

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:05.520
of significant
mosquito repellence.

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:07.920
No surprise given
that even if you slather

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:09.600
garlic oil on your skin,

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:13.200
within 30 minutes mosquitoes
don’t seem to care.

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:17.600
Eating garlic may,
however, help against ticks.

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:20.800
Because military personnel can
sometimes be at particularly high risk

00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:23.280
for tick bites
and tick-borne diseases,

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:26.160
the Swedish military
conducted randomized

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:27.840
controlled double-blind trials

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:32.400
of garlic to prevent
tick bites among marines.

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:35.360
Fifty swallowed the equivalent
of about a clove a day of garlic,

00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:38.560
and fifty took placebo pills;
then, they all switched.

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:42.560
And, there was a significant
reduction in tick bites

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:45.280
when consuming garlic
compared with placebo,

00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:49.505
cutting the risk of tick bites
by about 20 percent.

00:04:49.505 --> 00:04:51.840
Twenty percent is better than nothing,
but treating your clothing

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:54.080
with something like
permethrin has been shown

00:04:54.080 --> 00:04:56.560
to be 100 percent effective
against deer ticks,

00:04:56.560 --> 00:04:58.720
the vector of Lyme disease, and so,

00:04:58.720 --> 00:05:04.080
that may be better than counting
on garlic bread to save you.

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:08.000
An online survey of personal
mosquito-repellent strategies

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:10.720
unearthed all sorts
of strange stratagems

00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:14.560
from cutting a tomato in half
and leaving it next to the bed,

00:05:14.560 --> 00:05:18.800
to rubbing yourself with cigarette
butts soaked in alcohol.

00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:22.160
You could also soak yourself
in alcohol with a gin and tonic,

00:05:22.160 --> 00:05:26.320
cut down on sugar,
cut out meat, or bananas.

00:05:26.320 --> 00:05:28.960
But what’s funny
is some say avoid bananas,

00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:31.160
some say eat bananas
to stave off mosquitos,

00:05:31.160 --> 00:05:35.120
and others say topically
rub them on your skin.

00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:39.520
(I don’t even want to know about
what else you might do with them).

00:05:39.520 --> 00:05:41.440
Eat bananas? Avoid bananas?

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:46.480
You don’t know...until
you put it to the test.

00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:49.520
The impact of the consumption
of bananas

00:05:49.520 --> 00:05:53.040
on attraction of a malaria
mosquito to humans.

00:05:53.040 --> 00:05:56.160
Researchers in Wisconsin
randomized subjects to eat grapes

00:05:56.160 --> 00:06:01.200
or bananas before testing what they
then tasted like to mosquitoes and...

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:05.200
the ingestion of bananas was strongly
associated with an increase

00:06:05.200 --> 00:06:08.960
in the number of mosquito contacts
for hours after eating a banana,

00:06:08.960 --> 00:06:11.040
translating to about
11 extra contacts

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:14.640
after 1 hour and 7 contacts
after 2 hours.

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:18.080
Meanwhile, the ingestion
of grapes had no effect.

00:06:18.080 --> 00:06:19.680
Interestingly, it didn’t
seem to matter

00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:21.680
how many bananas
you eat at once,

00:06:21.680 --> 00:06:23.440
as eating three bananas
didn’t appear

00:06:23.440 --> 00:06:26.480
to make people any tastier
than eating one.

00:06:26.480 --> 00:06:29.280
Bottomline, after years
of repeat experiments,

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:33.040
they concluded that ingestion
of bananas, but not grapes,

00:06:33.040 --> 00:06:36.000
resulted in a significantly higher
attraction of mosquitoes

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:38.240
for hours after
ingestion on average,

00:06:38.240 --> 00:06:42.640
though some people did appear
to be immune to the banana effect.

00:06:42.640 --> 00:06:44.480
What about that gin and tonic?

00:06:44.480 --> 00:06:47.520
Some have suggested alcohol
will keep the bugs away;

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:50.080
others suggest we might
suffer more mosquito bites

00:06:50.080 --> 00:06:51.920
after ingesting liquor.

00:06:51.920 --> 00:06:55.280
Well, there’s only
one way to find out.

00:06:55.280 --> 00:07:00.160
Drink some beer and put your arm
in a box with some mosquitoes.

00:07:00.160 --> 00:07:02.960
The title of the study
gives it away.

00:07:02.960 --> 00:07:06.640
Alcohol ingestion stimulates
mosquito attraction.

00:07:06.640 --> 00:07:09.040
About 40 percent of the mosquitoes
landed on their arms

00:07:09.040 --> 00:07:10.720
before drinking
a bottle of beer

00:07:10.720 --> 00:07:13.280
compared to about
50 percent afterwards.

00:07:13.280 --> 00:07:15.760
And it didn’t appear to have
anything to do with changes

00:07:15.760 --> 00:07:18.960
in sweat production or skin
temperature after the booze.

00:07:18.960 --> 00:07:22.400
It might just be something you
start exuding after you drink.

00:07:22.400 --> 00:07:25.840
And so, it’s also not just because
you got bit a bunch of times

00:07:25.840 --> 00:07:28.640
because you like blacked out
in an alley or something.

00:07:28.640 --> 00:07:31.600
The researchers concluded that
since the percent of mosquitos

00:07:31.600 --> 00:07:35.280
landing on volunteers significantly
increased after beer ingestion,

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:38.720
drinking alcohol stimulates
mosquito attraction.

00:07:38.720 --> 00:07:42.720
But it could just be drinking beer,
not necessarily all alcohol.

00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:44.640
And they only tested
one species of mosquito,

00:07:44.640 --> 00:07:49.440
the one that transmits diseases like
dengue, zika, and yellow fever.

00:07:49.440 --> 00:07:53.360
What about gauging human attractiveness
to malaria mosquitoes,

00:07:53.360 --> 00:07:56.160
before and after volunteers
consumed either beer

00:07:56.160 --> 00:07:59.840
(using 25 volunteers
and a total of 2500 mosquitoes)

00:07:59.840 --> 00:08:02.160
or water as a control,

00:08:02.160 --> 00:08:06.640
with 18 volunteers and only
about 1800 mosquitoes. And..?

00:08:06.640 --> 00:08:08.640
Water consumption had no effect,

00:08:08.640 --> 00:08:12.800
but beer consumption increased
the attractiveness of the volunteers.

00:08:12.800 --> 00:08:15.520
It’s like the mosquitos
put on beer goggles.

00:08:15.520 --> 00:08:19.280
Beer consumption increases
human attractiveness.

00:08:19.280 --> 00:08:22.880
To date, bananas and beer
are the only dietary components

00:08:22.880 --> 00:08:26.160
that have been shown
to increase mosquito attraction.

00:08:26.160 --> 00:08:29.600
OK, but we want to repel
mosquitos, not attract them.

00:08:29.600 --> 00:08:31.200
What about testing
B vitamins

00:08:31.200 --> 00:08:33.520
as a home remedy
against mosquitoes?

00:08:33.520 --> 00:08:36.640
B vitamins are often
recommended in the popular media

00:08:36.640 --> 00:08:41.200
as a systemic repellent against
mosquitoes, especially on the web.

00:08:41.200 --> 00:08:44.240
The first studies date
back over a half century.

00:08:44.240 --> 00:08:47.520
They gave people doses
of vitamins B1 and B6,

00:08:47.520 --> 00:08:49.840
yet in a series of subsequent
tests the mosquitoes

00:08:49.840 --> 00:08:53.280
probed avidly
and bit promptly.

00:08:53.280 --> 00:08:55.200
They only tested
the vitamins on four people,

00:08:55.200 --> 00:08:57.120
but the vitamins
appeared so useless,

00:08:57.120 --> 00:08:58.560
they didn’t think
it worth repeating

00:08:58.560 --> 00:09:00.640
the experiments in view
of the obvious lack

00:09:00.640 --> 00:09:04.000
of repellency
of these vitamins.

00:09:04.000 --> 00:09:07.600
We believe, the researchers
wrote, that we have disproved

00:09:07.600 --> 00:09:11.520
the notions that vitamins taken
orally act as mosquito repellents.

00:09:11.520 --> 00:09:14.800
But they only tested
vitamins B1 and B6.

00:09:14.800 --> 00:09:18.000
What about B-2, B-3,
B-5, B-9, B12?

00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:22.240
You don’t know...until
you put it to the test.

00:09:22.240 --> 00:09:24.720
These are the results of a small
number of published studies

00:09:24.720 --> 00:09:28.560
like the one I showed that suggested
that vitamin B complex supplements

00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:31.920
are not effective as repellents,
but these studies were limited

00:09:31.920 --> 00:09:34.160
by the use of very
few human subjects,

00:09:34.160 --> 00:09:38.080
only 1 species of mosquito,
and a limited number of B vitamins.

00:09:38.080 --> 00:09:41.520
So, researchers decided to put
it to rest once and for all.

00:09:41.520 --> 00:09:46.960
B-1, B-2, B-3, B-5, B-6, B-7,
also known as biotin, B-9, B12.

00:09:46.960 --> 00:09:50.560
They also just tried
a mega-dose of thiamine, B1,

00:09:50.560 --> 00:09:55.440
which is rumored to be the most
effective B and...nothing.

00:09:55.440 --> 00:09:57.840
No effect of vitamin B
supplementation.

00:09:57.840 --> 00:09:59.920
Any of them.
I mean it would be nice

00:09:59.920 --> 00:10:02.560
to have an effective
oral insect repellent.

00:10:02.560 --> 00:10:07.280
Unfortunately, vitamin B1
has been proved to be ineffective.

00:10:07.280 --> 00:10:09.760
And this includes so-called
mosquito repellent patches,

00:10:09.760 --> 00:10:12.400
that supposedly deliver
B1 through the skin.

00:10:12.400 --> 00:10:14.880
No protection
provided whatsoever.

00:10:14.880 --> 00:10:18.240
Bottomline: Vitamin B1,
also known as thiamine,

00:10:18.240 --> 00:10:21.680
is not a systemic
mosquito repellent in men.

00:10:21.680 --> 00:10:24.800
They also tried smearing
it on people’s skin.

00:10:24.800 --> 00:10:26.720
With failure after failure,

00:10:26.720 --> 00:10:29.200
you start feeling bad
for the volunteers,

00:10:29.200 --> 00:10:31.600
seated in a room
with their shirts off.

00:10:31.600 --> 00:10:34.960
Then, they just release
100 suckers into the room.

00:10:34.960 --> 00:10:36.480
Look at some of those bite rates.

00:10:36.480 --> 00:10:41.200
These are bites per minute.
Up to 96 bites per minute.

00:10:41.200 --> 00:10:43.040
Just think how bad
it would have been

00:10:43.040 --> 00:10:46.907
if they had had some
bananas and beer.

